# Github Workflows
This folder contains files that GitHub uses to run CI/CD (referred to as Actions or Workflows). The workflows are typically used for infrastructure deployments, code deployment.

## Workflows
The workflows are designed to deploy infrastructure as well as code at the same time. This provides an IaC way of working in case any changes are made to code as well as infrastructure the changes will automatically be deployed together.
To keep the workflows condensed into one single workflow we have decided to keep the workflows in a repository of their own.
This does not stop you from creating your own workflows and implementing them within this repository.

To correctly configure the workflow you will need to change the environments that are being used. 
To do this all you need to do in the code is match the environment environment: 'dv' to the environments that you chose. It is also a good idea to change the options to the environments that were chosen as well.

## Overview

Workflows are located under `.github/workflows` and are responsible for:

- Infrastructure provisioning using Terraform
- Application build (backend & frontend)
- Application deployment to Azure services
- Optional cleanup (destroy workflows)

---

## Available Workflows (Aligned with Repo)

- - iac.yaml  
  Performs Terraform validation / checks

- infrastructure-deploy.yml  
  Deploys Azure infrastructure using Terraform (iac folder)

- infrastructure-destroy.yml  
  Destroys deployed infrastructure (non-prod scenarios)


---

## How Workflows are Designed

Workflows are structured to support **Infrastructure as Code (IaC)** and **application deployment** in a coordinated manner.

- Infrastructure (iac/) is deployed using Terraform
- Application (src/) is built and deployed using separate workflows
- Pipelines are modular (not a single combined workflow), allowing flexibility and control

---

## Environment Configuration

Workflows use GitHub Environments (e.g., dv).

To configure:
- Update the `environment` field in workflow files (example: dv)
- Ensure the same environment exists in GitHub Settings
- Ensure matching folder exists in `environments/<env>/`

---

## Manual Execution (GitHub UI)

You can manually trigger workflows:

1. Go to **GitHub Repository**
2. Click **Actions**
3. Select a workflow:
   - infrastructure-deploy
   - build-backend / build-frontend
   - backend-deployment / frontend-deployment
4. Click **Run workflow**
5. Select:
   - Branch
   - Environment (if applicable)
6. Click **Run**

---

## Key Points

- Infrastructure and application pipelines are separated
- Terraform uses values from environments folder
- Workflows rely on GitHub Environments for secrets & variables
- Manual triggering is supported for all workflows
